From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2_codec2: take the single-planar encoder input height from
 the format

The encoder sizes the frames of its input format convertor by codedSize(),
which configureInputFormat() takes from
V4L2Device::allocatedSizeFromV4L2Format(). That function derives the coded
height from sizeimage (sizeimage * 8 / width / bits per pixel). When the
device pads sizeimage beyond the frame, the height comes out too large:
Qualcomm iris rounds the NV12 input size up to whole 4 KiB pages, so 640x480
(sizeimage 462848) becomes 640x482.

The convertor then allocates 640x482 NV12 buffers. minigbm aligns their luma
plane to 512 rows and puts the chroma plane after it, while the device,
following the V4L2 single-planar layout, reads the chroma at
bytesperline x height = 640 x 480. Every converted frame (RGB input such as
screen recording or GL-rendered surfaces) is encoded with its chroma taken
from 32 rows above where it was written: the top 64 rows turn green and the
colours of the whole picture move down. Byte-buffer input and NV12 surfaces
are not converted and are not affected.

On iris the frame falls 2048 bytes short of a whole number of pages when the
stride is an odd multiple of 128 and the 32-aligned height an odd multiple of
32. The derived height only grows when those 2048 bytes make up at least one
row, i.e. for strides up to 1152: 640x480, 800x480, 854x480, 352x288,
120x160 and the like. Wider frames such as 1920x1440 are padded as well but
come out at the right height.

For single-planar formats use the format's height, which is where the device
expects the chroma to start. Multi-planar formats pass every plane with its
own offset and keep the previous size. The convertor frames still cover
sizeimage: minigbm pads NV12 by 12 KiB and rounds up to 4 KiB, more than
iris adds.

Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 v4l2/V4L2Encoder.cpp | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/v4l2/V4L2Encoder.cpp b/v4l2/V4L2Encoder.cpp
index 38ec2ce..172c9ba 100644
--- a/v4l2/V4L2Encoder.cpp
+++ b/v4l2/V4L2Encoder.cpp
@@ -508,6 +508,15 @@ bool V4L2Encoder::configureInputFormat(VideoPixelFormat inputFormat, uint32_t st
     // Calculate the input coded size from the format.
     // TODO(dstaessens): How is this different from mInputLayout->coded_size()?
     mInputCodedSize = V4L2Device::allocatedSizeFromV4L2Format(*format);
+    // In a single-planar format the chroma planes follow the luma plane directly, so the device
+    // reads them at bytesperline x height. allocatedSizeFromV4L2Format() derives the height from
+    // sizeimage instead, which overshoots when the device pads the image size: iris rounds it up
+    // to whole pages, and 640x480 NV12 comes out as 640x482. The format convertor allocates its
+    // frames at this size, and gralloc can then place their chroma further down than the device
+    // reads it (minigbm aligns the 482 rows to 512). Use the format's height for these formats.
+    if (!mInputLayout->mMultiPlanar) {
+        mInputCodedSize.set(mInputCodedSize.width, mInputLayout->mCodedSize.height);
+    }
 
     // Configuring the input format might cause the output buffer size to change.
     auto outputFormat = mOutputQueue->getFormat();
